e United States. It designs, builds, markets, and sells condominiums through Toll Brothers City Living. The company also develops a range of single-story living and first-floor primary bedroom suite home designs, as well as communities with recreational amenities, such as golf courses, marinas, pool complexes, country clubs, and fitness and recreation centers; and develops, operates, rents apartments and student housing communities. In addition, it provides various interior fit-out options, such as flooring, wall tile, plumbing, cabinets, fixtures, appliances, lighting, and home-automation and security technologies. Further, the company owns and operates architectural, engineering, mortgage, title, land development, insurance, smart home technology, landscaping, lumber distribution, house component assembly, and component manufacturing operations. It serves luxury first-time, move-up, empty-nester, active-adult, and second-home buyers. The company was founded in 1967 and is headquartered in Fort Washington, Pennsylvania.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Karl K. Mistry | CEO & Director | Age 44","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 4K people. Revenue per employee of $2.2M ranks in the top quartile among Consumer Cyclical peers, with revenue growing 7%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $209K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $6K per employee reflects an ownership culture. Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (3/10).","financial_summary":["Toll Brothers, Inc. is a large-cap consumer cyclical business with a $13.1B market cap on $11.0B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +2% over the past three years, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Returns on capital run at 25% against a 11% cost of capital \u2014 a 14-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat.","The business throws off $1.0B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 9 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, with adequate 0.83\u00d7 cash conversion.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year, and interest coverage of 40\u00d7 is fortress-like.","Management returns a meaningful 5.7% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome, and within its sector the business captures 2.0\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Piotroski score 3/9 \u2014 the fundamentals are deteriorating on multiple fronts","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Priced near or above estimated fair value, so the margin of safety has been bid away","sev":"amber","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.11672140314409075},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 5.6% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Heavy insider selling (net $26.1M)","sev":"amber","cat":"insider","dollars":-26050702.53},{"text":"Financial quality metrics lag Consumer Cyclical peers","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Cyclical sectors like Consumer Cyclical tend to underperform in slowdowns","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Consumer staples outperforming discretionary, signaling cautious spending","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Brand relevance decay \u2014 shifting consumer preferences and social-media-driven trends can strand inventory and invalidate positioning faster than product cycles can adapt.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Operating margins 8% above sect